/* Text selection on the surfaces mono repaints. Same two-lane wiring as the
   scrollbar above, and for the same reason. */
/* ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Text selection on mono's painted surfaces — ONE design, defined once.

   The browser's default selection swaps in its own background (a light blue in
   Chrome) and leaves the text `color` alone. That is fine wherever the page owns
   the ink. It is not fine on a surface mono has repainted: a navy sidebar puts
   white text on that pale blue and the label disappears.

   Before this file there was no `::selection` rule anywhere in the repository —
   source or built — so the UA default was unconditionally in play.

   ── Why `currentColor`, and NOT the `-contrast` token ──────────────────────
   The ink is not always light. sidebar.css:44 spells it out: it follows
   `--theme-<color>-contrast`, and `--theme-warning-contrast` is
   `rgba(0,0,0,.87)` — an amber sidebar has near-BLACK text. A rule that
   hardcoded a pale wash would be wrong there in precisely the way the UA default
   is wrong on navy, just in the other direction.

   So the wash is a low-alpha mix of the surface's OWN ink — the same device the
   shared scrollbar uses for its thumb, for the same reason. Two consequences:

     · `color` is deliberately NOT set. The text keeps whatever ink its surface
       gave it, so the pair can never disagree — which is the entire failure this
       file exists to fix.
     · the wash is a tint of that ink, so it comes out pale on navy and dark on
       amber with no colour named here, no per-component override, and no
       per-flavor rule. A new theme, flavor or `color` preset is right for free.

   ── Two traps ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   1. `::selection` accepts only a handful of properties, and the `background`
      SHORTHAND is not one of them. It has to be `background-color`, or the rule
      parses and then silently does nothing.

   2. `currentColor` inside a highlight pseudo-element resolves to that
      highlight's own colour which — because `color` is left unset above —
      inherits from the originating element. That is what makes it the surface's
      ink rather than the UA's highlight ink. The plain rgba that precedes it is
      a deliberate fallback: an engine that refuses the mix drops only the second
      declaration, so a neutral grey wash still lands and stays legible against a
      light AND a dark ink, instead of the whole rule falling out and restoring
      the bug.

   ── Scope ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Only the surfaces mono repaints. Cards, modals and table cells are left to the
   UA on purpose: mono has not touched their ink, so the default is already
   correct there, and a component library has no business restyling selection
   across a host page it does not own.

   Both the root class and the inner panel class are listed. The light build puts
   the root class on the host element; the shadow build's host is
   `<mono-shadow-…>` and `toShadowCss()` rewrites only the ELEMENT selector to
   `:host`, leaving `.mono-sidebar` unmatched inside the root — the inner classes
   are what carry the rule in that lane.
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
.mono-sidebar::selection,
.mono-sidebar ::selection,
.mono-sidebar-panel::selection,
.mono-sidebar-panel ::selection,
.mono-nav::selection,
.mono-nav ::selection,
.mono-menu::selection,
.mono-menu ::selection,
.mono-button::selection,
.mono-button ::selection {
  background-color: rgba(127, 127, 127, 0.42);
  background-color: color-mix(in srgb, currentColor 30%, transparent);
}
/*
 * Client-only loading skeleton. The `mono-client-only` Vite plugin injects a
 * flex container of gray "bars" as the <ClientOnly> fallback:
 *
 *   client-skeleton-type   'col' (default) | 'row'
 *   client-skeleton-bar    utility classes applied to EACH bar (e.g. "w-full h-20")
 *   client-skeleton-count  number of bars (default 5)
 *   client-skeleton-class  utility classes applied to the container
 *
 * Layout (7px gap between bars):
 *   col — vertical; bars stretch full width unless `client-skeleton-bar` sets one
 *   row — horizontal; bar size comes from `client-skeleton-bar`
 *
 * Shipped in `@mono-lit/helper/ui/index.css` (via src/entries/index.css), so any
 * consumer of that stylesheet gets the skeleton styling automatically.
 */
.mono-client-skeleton {
  display: flex;
  gap: 7px;
}
.mono-client-skeleton--col {
  flex-direction: column;
}
.mono-client-skeleton--row {
  flex-direction: row;
  align-items: flex-start;
}
/* A single loading bar. Colours come from the theme tokens (same hooks as the automatic
   `pending` skeleton, see components/skeleton/skeleton.css), so the fallback follows
   every flavor and `.dark` instead of a hard-coded light gray. */
.mono-client-skeleton-bar {
  position: relative;
  flex: none;
  overflow: hidden;
  isolation: isolate;

  --shimmer-bg: var(--mono-skeleton-bg, var(--mono-mode-surface-hover, var(--muted, rgb(226 232 240))));
  --shimmer-color: var(
    --mono-skeleton-color,
    color-mix(in oklab, var(--mono-mode-surface-strong, var(--foreground, #000)) 22%, transparent)
  );
  --shimmer-duration: var(--mono-skeleton-duration, 1.5s);

  border-radius: var(--mono-skeleton-radius, 4px);
  background: var(--shimmer-bg);
}
/* Default size: full width, 30px tall. Wrapped in :where() so it has ZERO
   specificity — any `client-skeleton-bar` utility (e.g. w-40, h-8) overrides it. */
:where(.mono-client-skeleton-bar) {
  width: 100%;
  height: 30px;
}
/* Shimmer sweep — phantom-ui's `.shimmer-block::after`, so a <ClientOnly> fallback bar
   and the automatic `pending` skeleton read as one and the same placeholder. */
.mono-client-skeleton-bar::before {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  z-index: 1;

  content: "";

  background: linear-gradient(
    90deg,
    var(--shimmer-bg) 30%,
    var(--shimmer-color) 50%,
    var(--shimmer-bg) 70%
  );
  background-size: 200% 100%;
  animation: mono-shimmer-horizontal var(--shimmer-duration) linear infinite;
}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .mono-client-skeleton-bar::before {
    display: none;
  }
}
/* The automatic skeleton (`pending` + phantom-ui) — theme hooks of the wrapper. */
/*
 * The automatic skeleton (`pending`, see src/composables/mono-skeleton.ts).
 *
 * Every mono element that renders wrapped draws `<phantom-ui mono-skeleton>` around its
 * content and only toggles `loading`. phantom-ui (the optional peer
 * `@aejkatappaja/phantom-ui`) measures the element's real DOM and overlays shimmer
 * blocks; these rules make that overlay FOLLOW THE MONO THEME and cost nothing while
 * idle.
 *
 * Shipped in `@mono-lit/helper/ui/index.css` (via src/entries/index.css) and appended to
 * every shadow sheet by `toShadowCss()` — a shadow root never sees the global sheet.
 *
 * Theming hooks (set anywhere above the element, or on it):
 *   --mono-skeleton-bg        block colour        (default: the theme's muted surface)
 *   --mono-skeleton-color     sweep colour        (default: a translucent foreground wash)
 *   --mono-skeleton-duration  cycle length        (default 1.5s)
 *   --mono-skeleton-radius    the wrapper's frame (default: the theme radius)
 * All defaults resolve from the theme tokens, which already change per flavor and under
 * `.dark` — so there is no separate dark block here, and no colour is hard-coded.
 */
phantom-ui[mono-skeleton] {
  /* phantom reads these three on its host; declaring them on the element (document
     level) beats its `:host` defaults, and they inherit into its shadow overlay. */
  --shimmer-bg: var(--mono-skeleton-bg, var(--mono-mode-surface-hover, var(--muted)));
  --shimmer-color: var(
    --mono-skeleton-color,
    color-mix(in oklab, var(--mono-mode-surface-strong, var(--foreground)) 22%, transparent)
  );
  --shimmer-duration: var(--mono-skeleton-duration, 1.5s);

  /* The wrapper only exists while loading (see mono-pending.ts); should one ever sit idle,
     it is not a box at all — phantom's own `:host { display: block; position: relative;
     overflow: hidden }` would otherwise turn an inline control into a block. */
  display: contents;
}
/* LOADING: become the box phantom measures against — the host's content size. */
phantom-ui[mono-skeleton][loading] {
  display: block;
  position: relative;
  overflow: hidden;
  box-sizing: border-box;
  max-width: 100%;
  border-radius: var(--mono-skeleton-radius, var(--mono-radius-md, var(--radius)));
}
/* Inline hosts keep their inline flow while loading (button.css etc. set the host to
   inline-block / inline-flex; a block wrapper inside would break the line). */
:is(mono-button, mono-chip, mono-checkbox, mono-radio, mono-switch, mono-dropdown, mono-status-dot)
  > phantom-ui[mono-skeleton][loading] {
  display: inline-block;
  vertical-align: middle;
  width: 100%;
}
:is(mono-button-dropdown, mono-table-detail, mono-table-checkbox)
  > phantom-ui[mono-skeleton][loading] {
  display: inline-flex;
}
/* NESTED pending (a card still loading around a table still loading): the OUTER
   skeleton is the one the user sees; the inner one goes transparent rather than
   shimmering through it. The vars inherit into phantom's shadow overlay. */
phantom-ui[mono-skeleton][loading] phantom-ui[mono-skeleton][loading] {
  --shimmer-color: transparent;
  --shimmer-bg: transparent;
}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  phantom-ui[mono-skeleton] {
    --shimmer-duration: 0s;
  }
}
/* ── CSS-only skeleton: the `mono-pending` attribute ──────────────────────────
   The JS-free half of the feature, for everything that has to look like a skeleton
   BEFORE any script runs or where phantom-ui has nothing to measure: server HTML,
   the hydration window, a plain `<div mono-pending>` bar. One vocabulary with the
   component prop / phantom options:

     <div mono-pending style="height:2rem"></div>
     <div mono-pending mono-pending-animation="pulse" mono-pending-mode="skeleton">…</div>

   `mono-pending-animation`  shimmer (default) | pulse | breathe | solid
   `mono-pending-mode`       skeleton (default: the element becomes one block, its
                             content hidden) | overlay (content stays, dimmed, swept)

   The mono elements set these on their HOST while `pending` resolves true, so an
   element paints as a block the instant it exists; once phantom-ui is defined the
   element also carries `mono-pending-covered` and this paint steps aside for
   phantom's measured blocks. Without phantom-ui this IS the skeleton. */
/* Every rule below mirrors phantom-ui's own stylesheet for its `.shimmer-block` (1.6.x:
   `.shimmer-block { background: var(--shimmer-bg) }`, its `::after` sweep, the
   `animation="pulse|breathe|solid"` variants, `mode="overlay"` and the reduced-motion
   fallback) — one block per host instead of one per measured leaf, but the same look. */
[mono-pending]:not([mono-pending="false"]):not([mono-pending-covered]) {
  /* phantom-ui's three knobs, resolved from the SAME mono tokens the wrapper above
     hands to phantom — so this paint and phantom's measured blocks are literally the
     same colours, the same gradient and the same clock. */
  --shimmer-bg: var(--mono-skeleton-bg, var(--mono-mode-surface-hover, var(--muted)));
  --shimmer-color: var(
    --mono-skeleton-color,
    color-mix(in oklab, var(--mono-mode-surface-strong, var(--foreground)) 22%, transparent)
  );
  --shimmer-duration: var(--mono-skeleton-duration, 1.5s);
  position: relative;
  overflow: hidden;
  isolation: isolate;
  /* phantom's `fallbackRadius` (4px) — the radius a measured leaf gets when it has none */
  border-radius: var(--mono-skeleton-radius, 4px);
  pointer-events: none;
  user-select: none;
  /* phantom hides text with -webkit-text-fill-color (see its #phantom-ui-loading-styles) */
  -webkit-text-fill-color: transparent !important;
  color: transparent !important;
  caret-color: transparent;
