   sees the HOST's child order, so `:first-child` there means "first child of the
   card element" — a body paragraph that follows a `slot="title"` heading is not
   first, and kept the page's margins while its light twin did not.

   `!important` on the slotted twins is not a style choice: a `::slotted()` rule
   is an INNER-tree declaration, and a normal declaration from the outer tree (a
   page's `p { margin: … }`) beats it at any specificity. Marking it important
   inverts that order. The light twins need no such thing — they are ordinary
   descendant rules in the same tree as the page. */
:where([mono-card]) > [mono-body] > * {
  margin-block: 0 !important;
}
:where([mono-card]) > [mono-body] slot::slotted(*) {
  margin-block: 0 !important;
