   alert dialog builds, which the controller does not produce. */
[mono-modal][mono-dialog] > [mono-panel-wrap] > [mono-panel] > [mono-body] {
  text-align: center;
}
/* basecoat@1.0.2 components/dialog.css .alert-dialog >> &:is([data-size='sm']) > * > footer, > [data-size='sm'] > footer
   — grid grid-cols-2. The controller's dialog is that `sm` alert: its actions sit
   together under the question rather than trailing to the end. */
[mono-modal][mono-dialog] > [mono-panel-wrap] > [mono-panel] > [mono-footer] {
  justify-content: center;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
}
/* The light build re-appends slotted nodes into a `[data-mono-slot]` outlet.
   Left inline, that outlet is ONE flex child, so the footer's gap and
   justification apply to IT rather than to the buttons inside it — while the
   shadow build's `<slot>` is `display: contents` per the UA sheet and the
   hand-written markup has no wrapper at all. Dissolving the outlet is what
   makes the three agree, and it is what a consumer writing
   `<button slot="footer">` twice expects. */
:where([mono-modal] > [mono-panel-wrap] > [mono-panel] > [mono-footer]) > [data-mono-slot] {
  display: contents;
}
/* The captured footer lands in an inline `<span data-mono-slot="foot">` outlet, so
   the buttons would otherwise sit in a LINE box — baseline-aligned, a solid button
   with an icon a few px lower than an outline one beside it, with line-height air
   underneath. The outlet becomes the flex row instead, and the actions wrapper
   dissolves into it. */
[mono-modal][mono-dialog] > [mono-panel-wrap] > [mono-panel] > [mono-footer] > [data-mono-slot="foot"] {
  display: flex;
  flex: 1 1 auto;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  gap: var(--_mono-modal-footer-gap);
  line-height: 1;
}
[mono-modal][mono-dialog] .mono-modal-dialog-actions {
  display: contents;
}
/* @unocss-include */
/* =========================================================================
   mono-nav — the top app bar.

   Basecoat ships NO nav component. basecoat-css@1.0.2 has dialog, drawer,
   sidebar, tabs, dropdown-menu … and no header bar, so this is an EXTENSION:
   the structure is mono's own and every VALUE is drawn from the Basecoat token
   layer and from `.sidebar nav`, which is the other piece of app chrome and the
   one this bar sits beside. Where a rule has an upstream counterpart it is
   cited; everything else is ours.

   Styled by ATTRIBUTE, like the rest of the port — and the attributes mirror
   the element's props one for one, so hand-written markup reads like the Lit /
   Vue tag:

     <mono-nav density="compact" color="primary" variant="outlined" extension>
     <header mono-nav mono-density="compact" mono-color="primary"
             mono-variant="outlined" mono-has-extension role="banner">
       <div mono-inner>
         <div mono-start>…</div>
         <div mono-center>…</div>
         <div mono-end>…</div>
       </div>
       <div mono-extension>…</div>
     </header>

   An absent attribute is the prop's default (`:not([mono-density])` =
   comfortable, `:not([mono-color])` = surface, `:not([mono-variant])` =
   elevated). `sticky` defaults to TRUE, so it is the NEGATIVE that says
   something: `mono-static` opts a bar out. The old classes
   (`.mono-nav.comfortable.surface.elevated.sticky`) are still emitted as inert
   hooks until 2.0 but no rule here reads them.

   Selector map:
     [mono-nav]            the <header> — the bar, and the var scope
     [mono-density=…]      EXTENSION (compact / comfortable / default)
     [mono-color=…]        EXTENSION (ten palette roles, `surface`, `custom`)
     [mono-variant=…]      EXTENSION (elevated / flat / outlined)
     [mono-inner]          the bar row
     [mono-start] / [mono-center] / [mono-end]   the three regions
     [mono-extension]      the optional second row

   COLOUR. `surface` (the default) leaves the bar unpainted: it takes
   `--sidebar` / `--sidebar-foreground` / `--sidebar-border`, exactly what
   upstream gives the sidebar's own chrome. Every other role PAINTS the bar in
   that role and inks it in the role's `-foreground` token, the way
   `.btn[data-variant='primary']` does — which is what finally retires the
   `--*-rgb` triples and the hand-rolled contrast maths the pre-port sheet
   needed. A literal `color` ("#7c3aed") still arrives inline from the component
   (`composables/color.ts`) as `mono-color="custom"`.

   NO GRADIENTS, NO BLUR. The pre-port `surface` bar was a 96%-opaque
   `backdrop-filter: blur(12px)` pane and `elevated` was an accent-tinted glow.
   Both are gone: the bar is opaque and `elevated` is `--mono-shadow-sm`.
   `--mono-nav-backdrop-filter` brings the blur back for a page that wants it.

   DARK MODE comes free — every colour here resolves through a Basecoat token
   that already flips, and the alphas are mode-independent. No `.dark` rule can
   live in component CSS anyway: a selector crosses neither the custom-element
   host nor the shadow boundary.

   FLAVORS set `--mono-nav-{bar-height,pad-x,gap,extension-height,radius,shadow,
   font-size,…}` (+ the per-density forms). Every fallback here is the token
   layer's own value.
   ========================================================================= */
mono-nav {
  display: block;
}
/* =========================================
   Root — the bar
   ========================================= */
[mono-nav] {
  /* ── the ten roles, each a public knob over a Basecoat token ───────────── */
  --_mono-nav-primary: var(--mono-nav-primary, var(--primary));
  --_mono-nav-secondary: var(--mono-nav-secondary, var(--secondary-foreground));
  --_mono-nav-success: var(--mono-nav-success, var(--success));
  --_mono-nav-danger: var(--mono-nav-danger, var(--destructive));
  --_mono-nav-warning: var(--mono-nav-warning, var(--warning));
  --_mono-nav-info: var(--mono-nav-info, var(--info));
  --_mono-nav-teal: var(--mono-nav-teal, var(--teal));
  --_mono-nav-purple: var(--mono-nav-purple, var(--purple));
  --_mono-nav-neutral: var(--mono-nav-neutral, var(--neutral));
  --_mono-nav-dark: var(--mono-nav-dark, var(--dark));

  /* the colour in play — `primary` unless a [mono-color] rule re-points it */
  --_mono-nav-accent: var(--mono-nav-accent, var(--_mono-nav-accent-preset, var(--_mono-nav-primary)));
  /* Ink for anything painted IN the accent. Each role points this at its own
     `-foreground` token, so a light role (warning, neutral) gets readable ink
     without a luminance calculation; a literal colour gets one written inline
     by the component. */
  --_mono-nav-on-accent: var(--mono-nav-on-accent, var(--_mono-nav-on-accent-preset, var(--primary-foreground)));

  /* ── the unpainted surface ───────────────────────────────────────────────
     basecoat@1.0.2 styles/vega.css .sidebar nav — bg-sidebar text-sidebar-foreground
     `--sidebar` IS `--card` in the token layer, so this is the card's surface
     under the name the one piece of upstream chrome actually uses. */
  --_mono-nav-surface: var(--mono-nav-surface, var(--sidebar));
  /* the `outlined` variant's bottom rule — a nav idiom with no card counterpart,
     so it takes the card's ring colour rather than inventing one */
  --_mono-nav-border: var(--mono-nav-border, var(--_mono-nav-ring-color));

  --_mono-nav-bg: var(--mono-nav-bg, var(--_mono-nav-bg-preset, var(--_mono-nav-surface)));
  --_mono-nav-text: var(--mono-nav-text, var(--_mono-nav-text-preset, var(--sidebar-foreground)));
  /* basecoat@1.0.2 styles/vega.css .sidebar nav h3 — text-sidebar-foreground/70 …:
     upstream's muted chrome ink is the bar ink at 70%, not a separate token. */
  --_mono-nav-text-soft: var(--mono-nav-text-soft, var(--_mono-nav-text-soft-preset, color-mix(in oklab, var(--_mono-nav-text) 70%, transparent)));
  /* basecoat@1.0.2 styles/vega.css .sidebar nav [role=separator] — border-sidebar-border mx-2 */
  --_mono-nav-border-lite: var(--mono-nav-border-lite, var(--_mono-nav-border-lite-preset, var(--sidebar-border)));

  /* ── comfortable metrics live on the unqualified root ON PURPOSE ───────── */
  --_mono-nav-height: var(--mono-nav-bar-height, var(--_mono-nav-height-preset, var(--mono-nav-bar-height-comfortable, calc(var(--mono-spacing) * 14))));
  --_mono-nav-extension-height: var(--mono-nav-extension-height, var(--_mono-nav-extension-height-preset, var(--mono-nav-extension-height-comfortable, calc(var(--mono-spacing) * 11))));
  /* the card's own gutter — basecoat@1.0.2 styles/vega.css .card > header — gap-1 rounded-t-xl px-6 …
     The compact and default steps SCALE from this one, so a flavor moves all
     three gutters by setting `--mono-nav-pad-x-comfortable` alone. */
  --_mono-nav-pad-unit: var(--mono-nav-pad-x-comfortable, calc(var(--mono-spacing) * 6));
  --_mono-nav-pad-x: var(--mono-nav-pad-x, var(--_mono-nav-pad-x-preset, var(--_mono-nav-pad-unit)));
  --_mono-nav-gap: var(--mono-nav-gap, var(--_mono-nav-gap-preset, var(--mono-nav-gap-comfortable, calc(var(--mono-spacing) * 3))));
  --_mono-nav-font: var(--mono-nav-font, var(--_mono-nav-font-preset, var(--mono-nav-font-comfortable, var(--mono-text-sm))));
  --_mono-nav-extension-font: var(--mono-nav-extension-font, var(--_mono-nav-extension-font-preset, var(--mono-nav-extension-font-comfortable, var(--mono-text-sm))));

  /* ── chrome — THE NAV IS A CARD ─────────────────────────────────────────
     basecoat@1.0.2 styles/vega.css .card — ring-foreground/10 bg-card
     text-card-foreground overflow-hidden rounded-xl text-sm shadow-xs ring-1 …

     Basecoat has no nav, but it has a wide rectangular surface with an edge and
     an elevation, and that is exactly what a top bar is. So the bar's chrome
     tracks `.card` value for value — radius, ring, shadow, gutter and type —
     and every flavor's nav delta is its CARD delta (sera square and roomy, maia
     2xl, luma 4xl with shadow-md, mira lg, lyra square and tight, rhea 3xl).
     Without this the eight styles all painted the same bar, which was the bug.

     The ring is drawn INSIDE the border box, as card.css draws it: an outside
     ring is the first pixel lost to any ancestor that clips, and a bar is
     usually flush against one. A full-bleed bar that wants no edge at all sets
     `--mono-nav-ring-width: 0`. */
  --_mono-nav-radius: var(--mono-nav-radius, var(--mono-radius-xl));
  --_mono-nav-ring-color: var(--mono-nav-ring-color, color-mix(in oklab, var(--foreground) 10%, transparent));
  --_mono-nav-ring-width: var(--mono-nav-ring-width, var(--mono-border-width));
  --_mono-nav-ring: inset 0 0 0 var(--_mono-nav-ring-width) var(--_mono-nav-ring-color);
  --_mono-nav-shadow: var(--mono-nav-shadow, var(--mono-shadow-xs));
  --_mono-nav-border-width: var(--mono-nav-border-width, var(--mono-border-width));
  --_mono-nav-backdrop-filter: var(--mono-nav-backdrop-filter, none);
  --_mono-nav-z: var(--mono-nav-z, 30);

  display: block;
  width: 100%;
  font-family: inherit;
  font-size: var(--_mono-nav-font);
  color: var(--_mono-nav-text);
  background: var(--_mono-nav-bg);
  border-radius: var(--_mono-nav-radius);
  border-bottom: var(--_mono-nav-border-width) solid transparent;
  backdrop-filter: var(--_mono-nav-backdrop-filter);
  -webkit-backdrop-filter: var(--_mono-nav-backdrop-filter);
  z-index: var(--_mono-nav-z);

  transition:
    background-color var(--mono-duration) var(--mono-ease),
    box-shadow var(--mono-duration) var(--mono-ease),
    border-color var(--mono-duration) var(--mono-ease);
}
[mono-nav],
:where([mono-nav]) *,
:where([mono-nav]) *::before,
:where([mono-nav]) *::after {
  box-sizing: border-box;
}
/* =========================================
   Density — EXTENSION: the bar's vertical rhythm
   ========================================= */
[mono-nav][mono-density="compact"] {
  --_mono-nav-height-preset: var(--mono-nav-bar-height-compact, calc(var(--mono-spacing) * 12));
  --_mono-nav-extension-height-preset: var(--mono-nav-extension-height-compact, calc(var(--mono-spacing) * 9));
  --_mono-nav-pad-x-preset: var(--mono-nav-pad-x-compact, calc(var(--_mono-nav-pad-unit) * 0.6667));
  --_mono-nav-gap-preset: var(--mono-nav-gap-compact, calc(var(--mono-spacing) * 2));
  --_mono-nav-font-preset: var(--mono-nav-font-compact, var(--mono-text-xs));
  --_mono-nav-extension-font-preset: var(--mono-nav-extension-font-compact, var(--mono-text-xs));
}
[mono-nav][mono-density="default"] {
  --_mono-nav-height-preset: var(--mono-nav-bar-height-default, calc(var(--mono-spacing) * 16));
  --_mono-nav-extension-height-preset: var(--mono-nav-extension-height-default, calc(var(--mono-spacing) * 12));
  --_mono-nav-pad-x-preset: var(--mono-nav-pad-x-default, calc(var(--_mono-nav-pad-unit) * 1.3333));
  --_mono-nav-gap-preset: var(--mono-nav-gap-default, calc(var(--mono-spacing) * 3.5));
  --_mono-nav-font-preset: var(--mono-nav-font-default, var(--mono-text-base));
  --_mono-nav-extension-font-preset: var(--mono-nav-extension-font-default, var(--mono-text-sm));
}
/* =========================================
   Colours — EXTENSION: `surface` is the bar unpainted, every role paints it
   ========================================= */
[mono-nav][mono-color="primary"] {
  --_mono-nav-accent-preset: var(--_mono-nav-primary);
  --_mono-nav-on-accent-preset: var(--primary-foreground);
}
[mono-nav][mono-color="secondary"] {
  --_mono-nav-accent-preset: var(--_mono-nav-secondary);
  --_mono-nav-on-accent-preset: var(--secondary);
}
[mono-nav][mono-color="success"] {
  --_mono-nav-accent-preset: var(--_mono-nav-success);
  --_mono-nav-on-accent-preset: var(--success-foreground);
}
[mono-nav][mono-color="danger"] {
  --_mono-nav-accent-preset: var(--_mono-nav-danger);
  --_mono-nav-on-accent-preset: var(--destructive-foreground);
}
[mono-nav][mono-color="warning"] {
  --_mono-nav-accent-preset: var(--_mono-nav-warning);
  --_mono-nav-on-accent-preset: var(--warning-foreground);
}
[mono-nav][mono-color="info"] {
  --_mono-nav-accent-preset: var(--_mono-nav-info);
  --_mono-nav-on-accent-preset: var(--info-foreground);
}
[mono-nav][mono-color="teal"] {
  --_mono-nav-accent-preset: var(--_mono-nav-teal);
  --_mono-nav-on-accent-preset: var(--teal-foreground);
}
[mono-nav][mono-color="purple"] {
  --_mono-nav-accent-preset: var(--_mono-nav-purple);
  --_mono-nav-on-accent-preset: var(--purple-foreground);
}
[mono-nav][mono-color="neutral"] {
  --_mono-nav-accent-preset: var(--_mono-nav-neutral);
  --_mono-nav-on-accent-preset: var(--neutral-foreground);
}
[mono-nav][mono-color="dark"] {
  --_mono-nav-accent-preset: var(--_mono-nav-dark);
  --_mono-nav-on-accent-preset: var(--dark-foreground);
}
/* A painted bar takes the role as its fill and the role's `-foreground` as its
   ink — the `.btn[data-variant='primary']` pattern, one level up. Every
   foreground on the bar is then mixed FROM that ink rather than from a literal
   white, so `warning` and `neutral` stay readable. `custom` is the marker for a
   literal `color`; its accent and ink arrive inline from the component. */
[mono-nav]:is(
    [mono-color="primary"],
    [mono-color="secondary"],
    [mono-color="success"],
    [mono-color="danger"],
    [mono-color="warning"],
    [mono-color="info"],
    [mono-color="teal"],
    [mono-color="purple"],
    [mono-color="neutral"],
    [mono-color="dark"],
    [mono-color="custom"]
  ) {
  --_mono-nav-bg-preset: var(--_mono-nav-accent);
  --_mono-nav-text-preset: var(--_mono-nav-on-accent);
  --_mono-nav-text-soft-preset: color-mix(in oklab, var(--_mono-nav-on-accent) 70%, transparent);
  --_mono-nav-border-lite-preset: color-mix(in oklab, var(--_mono-nav-on-accent) 20%, transparent);
}
/* =========================================
   Variants
   ========================================= */
/* `elevated` is the default, so the unqualified root carries it — and it is the
   card exactly: the inset ring AND the elevation, in that order. */
[mono-nav]:is(:not([mono-variant]), [mono-variant="elevated"]) {
  box-shadow: var(--_mono-nav-ring), var(--_mono-nav-shadow);
}
/* `flat` drops both. */
[mono-nav][mono-variant="flat"] {
  box-shadow: none;
  border-bottom-color: transparent;
}
/* `outlined` trades the elevation for a bottom rule — the nav idiom, and the
   one piece of its chrome the card has no counterpart for. The ring stays, so
   an outlined bar still reads as the same surface. */
[mono-nav][mono-variant="outlined"] {
  box-shadow: var(--_mono-nav-ring);
  border-bottom-color: var(--_mono-nav-border);
}
/* =========================================
   Sticky
   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   `sticky` is ON by default, so the attribute that says something is the
   negative. In the light build the host `<mono-nav>` is what sits in the parent
   layout flow and the inner `<header>` only fills it, so the host is what has to
   stick — a sticky inner header has no scroll distance inside a host that
   shrink-wraps it (which is also why leaving the rule on both is harmless). In
   hand-written markup the `<header>` IS the element in flow, and the same rule
   does the work.

   The host reflects the prop (`converter` empty-attr === true, missing ===
   false), so `[sticky]` is the right selector there.
   ========================================= */
mono-nav[sticky],
[mono-nav]:not([mono-static]) {
  position: sticky;
  top: 0;
  z-index: var(--mono-nav-z, 30);
}
[mono-nav][mono-static] {
  position: static;
}
/* =========================================
   The bar row
   ========================================= */
:where([mono-nav]) > [mono-inner] {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: var(--_mono-nav-gap);
  padding: 0 var(--_mono-nav-pad-x);
  height: var(--_mono-nav-height);
  min-height: var(--_mono-nav-height);
  width: 100%;
}
:where([mono-nav] > [mono-inner]) > [mono-start] {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: var(--_mono-nav-gap);
  flex-shrink: 0;
  min-width: 0;
}
:where([mono-nav] > [mono-inner]) > [mono-center] {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: var(--_mono-nav-gap);
  flex: 1;
  min-width: 0;
}
/* `margin-left: auto` keeps the end region at the trailing edge even when the
   center is empty and collapsed. */
:where([mono-nav] > [mono-inner]) > [mono-end] {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: calc(var(--_mono-nav-gap) * 0.7);
  flex-shrink: 0;
  margin-left: auto;
}
/* An empty region is NOT hidden.

   `:empty` cannot see across a shadow boundary: in the shadow build each region
   holds a <slot> ELEMENT, so the region is never `:empty` whether or not anything
   is assigned to it, and a <slot>'s own children are its fallback content rather
   than the assigned nodes — there is no selector for "nothing was slotted here".
   The light build WAS `:empty` in that case, so a bar with no center content laid
   out with one flex gap in the light build and two in the shadow one: 12px apart
   at max-content width, which is what a shrink-to-fit demo shows.

   Leaving every region in place is what makes the two builds agree. It costs
   nothing at a normal full-width bar — the center is `flex: 1` and eats the slack
   either way, and the end region is pinned by its own `margin-left: auto`. */
/* =========================================
   Extension row
   ========================================= */
:where([mono-nav]) > [mono-extension] {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: var(--_mono-nav-gap);
  padding: 0 var(--_mono-nav-pad-x);
  min-height: var(--_mono-nav-extension-height);
  border-top: var(--_mono-nav-border-width) solid var(--_mono-nav-border-lite);
  font-size: var(--_mono-nav-extension-font);
}
/* The element always renders the row so its slot target is stable; the root
   attribute is what turns it on. Hand-written markup that simply omits the row
   needs neither. */
[mono-nav]:not([mono-has-extension]) > [mono-extension] {
  display: none;
}
/* A slotted control inherits the bar's face, not the UA's. */
[mono-nav] :is(button, input, select, textarea) {
  font-family: inherit;
}
/* =========================================
   Responsive — a wide bar steps down a density on a narrow screen
   ========================================= */
@media (max-width: 639.98px) {
  [mono-nav][mono-density="default"] {
    --_mono-nav-height-preset: calc(var(--mono-spacing) * 14);
    --_mono-nav-pad-x-preset: calc(var(--mono-spacing) * 4);
    --_mono-nav-gap-preset: calc(var(--mono-spacing) * 2.5);
  }

  [mono-nav]:not([mono-density]) {
    --_mono-nav-height-preset: calc(var(--mono-spacing) * 12);
    --_mono-nav-pad-x-preset: calc(var(--mono-spacing) * 3.5);
    --_mono-nav-gap-preset: calc(var(--mono-spacing) * 2);
  }
}
/* =========================================
   Layout helper
   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Consumers wrap their main content in this to pad around the nav (and the
   sidebar). The vars are written on `:root` by mono-nav and mono-sidebar
   themselves. Kept as a CLASS — it is a consumer-applied utility, not a part of
   this component — with the attribute form accepted for markup that would
   rather stay attributes all the way down.
   ========================================= */
.mono-layout-content,
[mono-layout-content] {
  padding-top: var(--mono-nav-height, 0);
  padding-left: var(--mono-sidebar-left-width, 0);
  padding-right: var(--mono-sidebar-right-width, 0);
  min-height: 100vh;
  min-height: 100dvh;
  box-sizing: border-box;
}
/* =========================================
   Reduced motion
   ========================================= */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  [mono-nav] {
    transition: none;
  }
}
/* =========================================================================
   mono-radio — a port of Basecoat's `.input[type='radio']` and the `.label`
   beside it (basecoat-css@1.0.2, vega style).

   Styled by ATTRIBUTE, like Basecoat — and the attributes mirror the element's
   props one for one, so hand-written markup reads like the Lit / Vue tag:

     <mono-radio size="sm" color="danger" label="Email" value="email" model-value="email">
     <label mono-radio mono-size="sm" mono-color="danger">
       <input mono-input type="radio" name="contact" value="email" checked />
       <span mono-circle aria-hidden="true"></span>
       <span mono-label><span mono-label-text>Email</span></span>
     </label>

   An absent attribute is the prop's default (`:not([mono-size])` = md,
   `:not([mono-color])` = primary). The element renders these attributes on its
   root <label> (both builds) plus the STATE `mono-checked`; hand-written markup
   may rely on the native input's `:checked` instead — every state rule reads
   both. The old classes (`.mono-radio.sm.mono-radio-checked`) are still emitted
   as inert hooks until 2.0 but no rule here reads them.

   Selector map (ours ≡ upstream):
     [mono-radio]                                  ≡ .label (flex gap-2 text-sm font-medium leading-snug select-none)
     [mono-radio] > [mono-circle]                  ≡ .input[type='radio'] (size-4 rounded-full border-input; the real input is visually hidden)
     [mono-checked] / [mono-input]:checked         ≡ :checked (bg-primary border-primary) + :checked:before (size-2 bg-primary-foreground)
     [mono-circle] > [mono-dot] / ::before         ≡ :checked:before — the element renders the dot, raw markup may omit it
     [mono-input]:focus-visible + [mono-circle]    ≡ focus-visible:border-ring focus-visible:ring-3 focus-visible:ring-ring/50
     [mono-invalid] / [mono-input][aria-invalid]   ≡ aria-invalid:border-destructive aria-invalid:ring-destructive/20
     [mono-disabled] / [mono-input]:disabled       ≡ disabled:cursor-not-allowed disabled:opacity-50
     [mono-label-text]                             ≡ .label text
     [mono-description]                            ≡ .field > p (text-sm text-muted-foreground)
     [mono-size="xs|sm|lg|xl|xxl"]                 ≡ EXTENSION — the circle is 4/9 of the --mono-control-height-* step
                                                     (16px at vega md), label / description / gap step with it
     [mono-color="…"]                              ≡ EXTENSION (the checked fill in the role's colour)

   Every metric here is checkbox.css's: the two controls sit side by side in a
   form, so circle ≡ box, gap ≡ gap, label ≡ label at every step, and the
   pairing spec asserts it.

   Specificity contract (same as the pre-port class sheet): a part's RESTING rule
   is exactly one attribute strong — `:where([mono-radio]) [mono-circle]` =
   (0,1,0) — so a utility class handed in through `cssClass` wins by source
   order, while state rules (checked / focus / invalid / disabled, keyed on the
   root or the native input) stay heavier and win over it, as they always did.

   Inner parts: [mono-input], [mono-circle] > [mono-dot], [mono-label] >
   [mono-label-text] / [mono-description]. The shadow build marks an unassigned
   slot wrapper [mono-empty].

   The circle sits on the label's CAP BAND (see `--_mono-radio-line-offset`):
   mono's own alignment rule, kept — Basecoat centres a lone radio on its line
   box with `items-center`, which is the same thing for a one-line label and
   drifts for a described one.

   FLAVORS set `--mono-radio-{radius,shadow,ring-width,ring-alpha,bg,border-color,
   checked-bg,dot-color,size-md}` (the per-style `.input[type='radio']` deltas); every
   fallback here is vega's value (`node scripts/basecoat-styles.mjs --varying
   "input\[type='radio'\]"`).
   ========================================================================= */
mono-radio {
  display: inline-block;
}
[mono-radio] {
  /* ── palette: each slot is a public knob over a Basecoat token ─────────── */
  --_mono-radio-text: var(--mono-radio-text, var(--foreground));
  --_mono-radio-description: var(--mono-radio-description, var(--muted-foreground));
  --_mono-radio-primary: var(--mono-radio-primary, var(--primary));
  --_mono-radio-primary-foreground: var(--mono-radio-primary-foreground, var(--primary-foreground));
  --_mono-radio-accent: var(--mono-radio-accent, var(--_mono-radio-accent-preset, var(--_mono-radio-primary)));
  --_mono-radio-dot-color: var(--mono-radio-dot-color, var(--_mono-radio-dot-preset, var(--_mono-radio-primary-foreground)));
  --_mono-radio-border-color: var(--mono-radio-border, var(--mono-radio-border-color, var(--input)));
  --_mono-radio-bg: var(--mono-radio-bg, var(--mono-mode-surface));
  /* the radio is the one control Basecoat leaves shadowless (the checkbox has
     shadow-xs) — the slot stays so a flavor or a consumer can put one back */
  --_mono-radio-shadow: var(--mono-radio-shadow, 0 0 #0000);
  --_mono-radio-radius: var(--mono-radio-radius, var(--mono-radius-full));
  --_mono-radio-border-width: var(--mono-radio-border-width, var(--mono-border-width));
  --_mono-radio-ring-color: var(--mono-radio-ring-color, var(--ring));
  --_mono-radio-ring-width: var(--mono-radio-ring-width, var(--mono-ring-width));
  --_mono-radio-ring-alpha: var(--mono-radio-ring-alpha, var(--mono-ring-alpha));

  /* ── md metrics live on the unqualified root ON PURPOSE ────────────────────
     basecoat@1.0.2 styles/vega.css .input[type='radio'] — size-4 (= 4/9 of the
     h-9 control); .field > label, .label — gap-2 text-sm leading-snug */
  --_mono-radio-control: var(--mono-radio-size-md, calc(var(--mono-control-height-md) * 4 / 9));
  --_mono-radio-gap: var(--mono-radio-gap, var(--mono-radio-gap-md, calc(var(--mono-spacing) * 2)));
  --_mono-radio-label-font: var(--mono-radio-label-font-md, var(--mono-text-sm));
  --_mono-radio-description-font: var(--mono-radio-description-font-md, 0.8125rem);
  /* :checked:before — size-2 on a size-4 circle */
  --_mono-radio-dot: var(--mono-radio-dot, 50%);

  /* ── whole pixels ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     The circle is a ratio of the control step, so it lands on a fraction (18.39px,
     17.77px…). A fractional round shape is antialiased on every edge — the
     switch thumb, which parks flush against the track's cap, picked up a dark
     rim that changed with the flavor and the screen's pixel ratio. Snap the
     derived size to 1px; `round()` has the same browser floor as `color-mix()`
     and `:has()`, which this sheet already requires. Snapping happens HERE, at
     one place per value, so the per-size blocks stay plain arithmetic. */
  --_mono-radio-control-px: round(nearest, var(--_mono-radio-control), 1px);

  /* ── alignment with the label ────────────────────────────────────────────
     The circle is centred on the label's CAP BAND — the ink the eye reads, not
     the line box. Every number is checkbox.css's; the two controls sit side by
     side in a form and any divergence shows. */
  --_mono-radio-label-line-height: var(--mono-radio-label-line-height, var(--mono-leading-snug));
  --_mono-radio-optical-shift: 0.045;
  --_mono-radio-optical-nudge: 0px;
  --_mono-radio-line-offset: calc(
    (var(--_mono-radio-label-font) * var(--_mono-radio-label-line-height) - var(--_mono-radio-control-px)) / 2 +
      var(--_mono-radio-label-font) * var(--_mono-radio-optical-shift) + var(--_mono-radio-optical-nudge)
  );

  /* The element build nests this root inside its host, so it is never itself a
     grid or flex item and shrink-wraps its content. Hand-written markup usually
     IS the item (a radio in a `display: grid` list), and an item blockifies —
     `inline-flex` becomes `flex` and the row stretches to the widest sibling,
     wrapping its label at a different point than the element's. `fit-content`
     keeps both shapes shrink-wrapped; a consumer who wants a full-width row
     still sets a width. */
  width: fit-content;
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: flex-start;
  gap: var(--_mono-radio-gap);
  position: relative;
  vertical-align: top;
  cursor: pointer;
  user-select: none;
  font-family: inherit;
  color: var(--_mono-radio-text);
}
[mono-radio],
:where([mono-radio]) *,
:where([mono-radio]) *::before,
:where([mono-radio]) *::after {
  box-sizing: border-box;
}
:where([mono-radio]) :is([mono-label], [mono-label-text], [mono-description])[mono-empty] {
  display: none;
}
/* the circle seats on the first line of a label that is actually there; a bare
   circle stays flush at the top */
[mono-radio]:has([mono-label-text]:not([mono-empty])) > [mono-circle] {
  margin-top: var(--_mono-radio-line-offset, 0px);
}
/* =========================================
   Sizes — EXTENSION: the circle is 4/9 of the control step (vega md: 36 × 4/9 =
   16px), label, description and gap step with it (md = Basecoat's text-sm / gap-2)
   ========================================= */
